WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.525
Welcome to Module 5 of the Ticket-to-Machine
learning path: drift to a typed task.

2
00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:08.600
This module covers the
guide's Gates 3 and 4,

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.500
and the developer workbook's Chapter 04.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.650
In Module 4 you measured drift.

5
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.267
Now you follow one change from
SailPoint Identity Security Cloud,

6
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:21.625
ISC for short, into ServiceNow
as a typed finding.

7
00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:24.460
You'll learn what the
three ISC workflows send,

8
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:29.086
what the ServiceNow script does with
each payload, and how a small tool lets

9
00:00:29.086 --> 00:00:33.563
you see the exact payload on a laptop,
before any workflow runs in a tenant.

10
00:00:33.563 --> 00:00:37.350
Running that tool found two
gaps, and you'll see both fixes.

11
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.720
The code is illustrative, tested
against fictional fixtures.

12
00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:46.620
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.458
Picture it.

14
00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:51.191
Someone adds a proxy address to
an Active Directory account,

15
00:00:51.191 --> 00:00:53.058
outside the governed process.

16
00:00:53.058 --> 00:00:55.970
SailPoint's Native Change
Detection sees it,

17
00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.433
a workflow posts it to
ServiceNow, and a finding opens.

18
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:00.795
So far, so good.

19
00:01:00.795 --> 00:01:03.907
But suppose the workflow doesn't
send that kind of change.

20
00:01:03.907 --> 00:01:06.671
Then the finding's
intended state is empty.

21
00:01:06.671 --> 00:01:11.001
In Module 6, the re-read compares
the account with the intended state,

22
00:01:11.001 --> 00:01:14.482
and with nothing in it, there's
nothing to disagree with.

23
00:01:14.482 --> 00:01:16.582
The finding looks fixed when it isn't.

24
00:01:16.582 --> 00:01:21.875
That's why, in this module, you see the
exact payload each workflow produces,

25
00:01:21.875 --> 00:01:25.610
on a laptop, before any
workflow runs in a tenant.

26
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.175
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:34.798
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:34.798 --> 00:01:39.885
You'll describe the three ISC workflows
and what each event becomes in ServiceNow.

29
00:01:39.885 --> 00:01:43.710
You'll resolve a workflow's body
against a saved trigger input,

30
00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.835
and read the payload, the intended
state and the de-duplication key.

31
00:01:47.835 --> 00:01:51.947
You'll explain the two gaps the
tool found, and how each was fixed.

32
00:01:51.947 --> 00:01:55.522
And you'll verify that a closure
signal never closes a finding.

33
00:01:55.522 --> 00:01:57.954
Before you start, finish Module 4,

34
00:01:57.954 --> 00:02:02.547
and review the identity finding
contract from Module 1, Unit 3.

35
00:02:02.547 --> 00:02:07.010
Lab 1 needs only the kit, Python
and Node, with no tenant.

36
00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:12.330
Lab 2 needs a SailPoint sandbox tenant
and a ServiceNow developer instance.

37
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.880
The kit has one ISC workflow per trigger.

38
00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.048
A native change, where an account
was updated directly on the source,

39
00:02:23.048 --> 00:02:25.877
becomes an unauthorized
write on the account,

40
00:02:25.877 --> 00:02:30.343
and ServiceNow opens a finding whose
intended state reverts the change.

41
00:02:30.343 --> 00:02:34.783
An identity attribute change becomes
attribute drift on the identity,

42
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:37.417
and its intended state
keeps the new value.

43
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:41.967
A completed provisioning action is
a closure signal, and only a signal.

44
00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:46.593
It moves matching findings to Awaiting
verification; it never closes one.

45
00:02:46.593 --> 00:02:49.142
All three live in one workflow file.

46
00:02:49.142 --> 00:02:51.412
Each is a name, an owner, a trigger,

47
00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:55.516
and a definition whose one
action is an HTTP Request step,

48
00:02:55.516 --> 00:03:00.842
and every step posts to the same Scripted
REST resource, the one front door.

49
00:03:00.842 --> 00:03:05.629
Each is created disabled, because SailPoint
workflows can't be created enabled.

50
00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:08.580
You enable each one after a test run.

51
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:17.105
On the ServiceNow side, a Scripted
REST script receives every post.

52
00:03:17.105 --> 00:03:20.921
It validates the payload, sets
the priority and the due date,

53
00:03:20.921 --> 00:03:23.568
and de-duplicates against open findings.

54
00:03:23.568 --> 00:03:27.620
When the payload carries no
intended state, it derives one:

55
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.505
a native change is reverted,
an attribute change is kept.

56
00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:33.910
And on a successful provisioning signal,

57
00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.093
it moves matching findings
to Awaiting verification.

58
00:03:37.093 --> 00:03:40.992
The guide's Gate 4 validation
lists what the kit already checks.

59
00:03:40.992 --> 00:03:45.841
The contract test checks the workflow
file's shape, that it holds no secrets,

60
00:03:45.841 --> 00:03:49.517
and that every field its bodies
send is read by the script.

61
00:03:49.517 --> 00:03:52.574
The Node tests check
priority and due date,

62
00:03:52.574 --> 00:03:55.980
de-duplication, and that
a bad payload gets a 400.

63
00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.397
But none of it showed the payload itself,

64
00:03:58.397 --> 00:04:03.150
because SailPoint resolves the body's
dollar keys at run time, in a tenant.

65
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.500
So Chapter 04 adds a tool:
workflow payload dot p y.

66
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.154
It picks the workflow
for the trigger you name,

67
00:04:14.154 --> 00:04:17.925
and resolves the body's dollar keys
against a saved trigger input.

68
00:04:17.925 --> 00:04:21.675
It handles simple paths only,
like dollar dot a dot b.

69
00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:27.162
Anything more complex is refused,
because the tool isn't a JSONPath engine.

70
00:04:27.162 --> 00:04:31.742
Then it derives the intended state
exactly as the ServiceNow script does,

71
00:04:31.742 --> 00:04:34.671
checks the payload against
Module 1's contract,

72
00:04:34.671 --> 00:04:38.275
and lists the input's top-level
fields the body doesn't send.

73
00:04:38.275 --> 00:04:40.538
That last line is where gaps show up.

74
00:04:40.538 --> 00:04:45.290
Three fictional trigger inputs come with
it: a native change to Alice's account, a

75
00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.521
department change on Erin's identity,

76
00:04:47.521 --> 00:04:50.401
and a successful provisioning
action for Alice.

77
00:04:50.401 --> 00:04:53.785
One caution: the tool shows
what the body says to send,

78
00:04:53.785 --> 00:04:56.462
not what SailPoint's step actually sends.

79
00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.890
Lab 2 settles that for your tenant.

80
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:03.990
Here's the first gap.

81
00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.687
SailPoint's Native Change Account
Updated input has three kinds of change:

82
00:05:08.687 --> 00:05:13.156
single-value attribute changes,
entitlement changes, and multivalued

83
00:05:13.156 --> 00:05:17.853
attribute changes, each with a name,
the values added and the values removed.

84
00:05:17.853 --> 00:05:20.964
The workflow sent the first
two, but not the third.

85
00:05:20.964 --> 00:05:24.091
So a native change only to
a multivalued attribute,

86
00:05:24.091 --> 00:05:28.334
say a proxy address added out of
band, produced an intended state with

87
00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:32.502
nothing in it, and a re-read would
have found nothing to disagree with.

88
00:05:32.502 --> 00:05:35.503
That's exactly the story from
the start of this module.

89
00:05:35.503 --> 00:05:40.186
Now the workflow sends the multivalued
changes, and the script reverts them:

90
00:05:40.186 --> 00:05:45.241
values that were added must be absent, and
values that were removed must be present.

91
00:05:45.241 --> 00:05:50.270
Each fix went into the workflow or
script, the Python mirror, and a test.

92
00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.783
The second gap was in
the de-duplication key.

93
00:05:56.783 --> 00:06:01.170
The script de-duplicated on
source, subject and drift class.

94
00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.959
So if two attributes drifted on one user,

95
00:06:03.959 --> 00:06:07.508
say department and manager,
they became one record.

96
00:06:07.508 --> 00:06:10.364
The second finding's
intended state was lost,

97
00:06:10.364 --> 00:06:12.757
and the re-read checked only the first.

98
00:06:12.757 --> 00:06:16.595
Now the key adds the attribute
whenever the payload names one.

99
00:06:16.595 --> 00:06:20.845
You'll see it in the lab: Erin's finding
has a key that ends in department.

100
00:06:20.845 --> 00:06:24.937
Two attributes drifting on one
subject are now two findings,

101
00:06:24.937 --> 00:06:28.783
each with its own intended state,
and a node test pins that.

102
00:06:28.783 --> 00:06:33.050
The kit's README and the
implementation example both say so.

103
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.362
Chapter 04 is honest about what it
couldn't verify, and you should be too.

104
00:06:41.362 --> 00:06:45.055
The kit's trigger ID for a native
change isn't in SailPoint's

105
00:06:45.055 --> 00:06:48.825
published trigger table; the
page names only the event type.

106
00:06:48.825 --> 00:06:53.349
SailPoint documents the HTTP
Request step's fields in the editor,

107
00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:58.125
not as JSON, so the attribute names
in the kit's file are unconfirmed.

108
00:06:58.125 --> 00:07:02.880
Whether the step resolves the dollar
keys inside its JSON body, and whether

109
00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.338
the input sits at the root or under
trigger, are on the kit's verify list too.

110
00:07:07.338 --> 00:07:11.255
The shape of each added entitlement
isn't documented to that level,

111
00:07:11.255 --> 00:07:14.675
and neither are provisioning
results other than success.

112
00:07:14.675 --> 00:07:19.225
That's why the tool shows what the body
says to send, not what SailPoint sends.

113
00:07:19.225 --> 00:07:21.812
Lab 2 settles each of
these for your tenant,

114
00:07:21.812 --> 00:07:25.510
and the checklist asks you to
confirm them in a sandbox.

115
00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:34.773
Chapter 04 lists the dangers between an
event and a finding, and the guard for each.

116
00:07:34.773 --> 00:07:38.939
If the workflow doesn't send an
event field, part of the change is

117
00:07:38.939 --> 00:07:43.347
never reverted and the re-read
passes; the tool lists unsent fields.

118
00:07:43.347 --> 00:07:47.154
If two drifts fold into one
record, the second is lost;

119
00:07:47.154 --> 00:07:49.810
the attribute in the key prevents it.

120
00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:54.544
If someone edits a workflow in the editor
and not in the file, the repository

121
00:07:54.544 --> 00:07:58.972
stops describing what runs, so export
and diff after any editor change.

122
00:07:58.972 --> 00:08:02.165
Credentials must never
be in the workflow file;

123
00:08:02.165 --> 00:08:06.259
it uses placeholders, and the
contract test fails on a secret.

124
00:08:06.259 --> 00:08:09.685
And a closure signal must
never be treated as closure.

125
00:08:09.685 --> 00:08:12.729
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's point is that

126
00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:15.773
native tools change state;
they don't close findings.

127
00:08:15.773 --> 00:08:19.739
The script only moves a finding
to Awaiting verification,

128
00:08:19.739 --> 00:08:23.560
and Module 6's business rule
blocks any other close.

129
00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:28.805
Try it.

130
00:08:28.805 --> 00:08:34.430
Lab 1 runs on a laptop, in the kit's
servicenow-sailpoint folder, with no tenant.

131
00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:38.005
Step one runs the chapter's
tests: eleven pass.

132
00:08:38.005 --> 00:08:43.249
Step two resolves the native-change workflow
against Alice's fictional event, where

133
00:08:43.249 --> 00:08:48.193
her title was changed to Director, a group
was added, and a proxy address was added.

134
00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:50.155
Read the output line by line.

135
00:08:50.155 --> 00:08:54.142
It's a finding, an unauthorized
write on the ISC account.

136
00:08:54.142 --> 00:08:57.173
The intended state puts
the title back to Analyst,

137
00:08:57.173 --> 00:09:01.755
and says the proxy address and the
Payables Approvers group must be absent.

138
00:09:01.755 --> 00:09:07.242
The contract accepts it at priority 2,
with a key of source, account and class.

139
00:09:07.242 --> 00:09:10.196
And the only input field
not sent is the event type,

140
00:09:10.196 --> 00:09:14.880
which is always account updated for this
trigger, so dropping it loses nothing.

141
00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.470
Before the fix, the proxy
address was missing here.

142
00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:24.133
Step three is the mover.

143
00:09:24.133 --> 00:09:27.320
Erin's department moved
from HR to Finance.

144
00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:31.822
The finding is attribute drift on
her identity, and its intended

145
00:09:31.822 --> 00:09:36.408
state is the new value, department
Finance, to be kept, not reverted.

146
00:09:36.408 --> 00:09:40.933
It's priority 3, and the key now
ends in the attribute, department.

147
00:09:40.933 --> 00:09:42.970
No input field is dropped.

148
00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:46.908
Step four is the closure signal
for Alice's provisioning action.

149
00:09:46.908 --> 00:09:50.422
Its kind is closure signal,
the contract accepts it,

150
00:09:50.422 --> 00:09:55.358
and it doesn't send the action, the
requester, the sources or the warnings.

151
00:09:55.358 --> 00:10:00.383
A signal moves matching findings to
Awaiting verification; it never closes one.

152
00:10:00.383 --> 00:10:03.027
Step five runs the two new script tests,

153
00:10:03.027 --> 00:10:08.482
two attributes as two findings and
multivalued changes reverted, and both pass.

154
00:10:08.482 --> 00:10:12.695
On the slide, the workflow name
lines and the test names are trimmed.

155
00:10:12.695 --> 00:10:14.620
Check your work: for each event,

156
00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:18.820
say what ServiceNow receives
and what the re-read will check.

157
00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:27.366
Lab 2 takes this into your own test
instances: a SailPoint sandbox tenant,

158
00:10:27.366 --> 00:10:31.043
the ServiceNow developer
instance from Modules 1 and 2,

159
00:10:31.043 --> 00:10:33.920
and the workflow client
from Module 2's Lab 2.

160
00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.045
Never production.

161
00:10:35.045 --> 00:10:38.395
Turn on Native Change
Detection on one test source.

162
00:10:38.395 --> 00:10:41.632
Build one HTTP Request
step by hand, export it,

163
00:10:41.632 --> 00:10:45.307
and compare its attribute
names with the kit's file.

164
00:10:45.307 --> 00:10:49.827
Point it at a capture endpoint or your
developer instance, change a monitored

165
00:10:49.827 --> 00:10:54.345
attribute on a test account, and confirm
the payload matches the tool's output.

166
00:10:54.345 --> 00:10:58.045
If it doesn't, the dollar keys
or the input's root differ.

167
00:10:58.045 --> 00:11:00.907
Repeat the change, and
it should de-duplicate.

168
00:11:00.907 --> 00:11:02.120
Then back it all out.

169
00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:06.211
The ServiceNow endpoint has to be
reachable from SailPoint's cloud,

170
00:11:06.211 --> 00:11:08.695
which the workbook marks as an inference.

171
00:11:08.695 --> 00:11:11.200
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

172
00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:16.337
The federal requirement.

173
00:11:16.337 --> 00:11:20.821
Chapter 04's table maps an out-of-process
write, raised as an owned,

174
00:11:20.821 --> 00:11:26.487
clocked finding, to NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three C M three,

175
00:11:26.487 --> 00:11:30.801
configuration change control,
and S I four, system monitoring.

176
00:11:30.801 --> 00:11:36.725
It maps a tested workflow change to C M
three and S A eleven, developer testing.

177
00:11:36.725 --> 00:11:39.641
The evidence an ISSO
keeps from this module:

178
00:11:39.641 --> 00:11:44.824
the ServiceNow finding with its origin
and raw event; the finding records, one

179
00:11:44.824 --> 00:11:50.250
per drifting attribute; and the pull request
and test run for any workflow change.

180
00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.875
One label carries over.

181
00:11:51.875 --> 00:11:56.775
Treating a native change as an unauthorized
write is the program's inference.

182
00:11:56.775 --> 00:12:01.407
And provenance drift on a claim in
active use is P1, but the workflow

183
00:12:01.407 --> 00:12:05.787
can't know whether a claim is in use,
so a person or a rule raises it.

184
00:12:05.787 --> 00:12:09.750
Before shipping, work through
Chapter 04's checklist.

185
00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.475
Check yourself with two questions.

186
00:12:15.475 --> 00:12:19.088
First: before the fix, what
did a native change to only

187
00:12:19.088 --> 00:12:23.037
a multivalued attribute, such
as a proxy address, produce?

188
00:12:23.037 --> 00:12:27.118
The answer is b: a finding whose
intended state named nothing,

189
00:12:27.118 --> 00:12:30.301
so a re-read would find
nothing to disagree with.

190
00:12:30.301 --> 00:12:33.262
The workflow didn't send
the multivalued changes.

191
00:12:33.262 --> 00:12:37.600
Second: why does the de-duplication
key now end in the attribute?

192
00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:38.737
The answer is a.

193
00:12:38.737 --> 00:12:42.934
Two attributes drifting on one
user were folded into one record,

194
00:12:42.934 --> 00:12:46.100
and the second finding's
intended state was lost.

195
00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.591
The module page has two more questions,

196
00:12:48.591 --> 00:12:53.610
on the mover's intended state and on
what the tool does with a complex path.

197
00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:58.185
To sum up.

198
00:12:58.185 --> 00:13:03.078
You saw the exact payload each ISC
workflow sends, and the intended state

199
00:13:03.078 --> 00:13:07.972
each finding carries: a native change
reverted, an attribute change kept.

200
00:13:07.972 --> 00:13:12.875
You saw the two fixes that keep a finding
from reaching the re-read with nothing to

201
00:13:12.875 --> 00:13:15.875
check: multivalued changes
now sent and reverted,

202
00:13:15.875 --> 00:13:18.509
and the attribute in
the de-duplication key.

203
00:13:18.509 --> 00:13:21.244
And you confirmed that
a closure signal moves a

204
00:13:21.244 --> 00:13:24.398
finding to Awaiting verification
but never closes it.

205
00:13:24.398 --> 00:13:28.072
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 04 checklist.

206
00:13:28.072 --> 00:13:33.527
Next is Module 6, actuation and the
re-read rule: how Gate 5 actuates by lane,

207
00:13:33.527 --> 00:13:36.617
and how Gate 6 closes
a finding only when a

208
00:13:36.617 --> 00:13:40.072
re-read of the target
shows the intended state.

209
00:13:40.072 --> 00:13:44.370
You'll watch the re-read client decide
six waiting findings on two days.
